For the fields below, exhaustively scan all evidence and populate every directly supported value. Otherwise use `null` for scalars and an empty collection for collections. Every collection element—and both the title and company of every position—must have subject-specific grounding. Ground normalized values with text containing the underlying explicit fact. Do not transfer facts from nearby text about another person, an organization, an event, or a document, and do not stop after finding the first supported collection value.

- **Age:** Extract only a numeric age explicitly stated for the person, including direct constructions such as “aged 42” or “42-year-old.” Do not calculate or update age from a birth date, publication date, school year, or career timeline. Approximate ranges do not support an exact age.

- **Birthday:** Extract an explicitly stated birth date or birthday. Normalize a complete date to `YYYY-MM-DD`. When the year is unknown but month and day are both explicit, use `yyyy-MM-DD`, zero-padding numeric components. Thus an explicit February 4 birthday with no year becomes `yyyy-02-04`. Do not infer missing month or day components or derive the date from age or chronology.

- **Height:** Extract only a measurement explicitly identified as the person’s height. Preserve the supported measurement according to the schema. Do not estimate height from photographs, comparisons, category descriptions, or measurements of another person.

- **Emails:** Find every complete address explicitly identified as the person’s own contact address and preserve each exactly as shown. Ground every address independently with both the address and its association to the person. Do not infer an address from a name or domain, reconstruct obfuscated text, or treat an address as personal merely because it appears in a message, header, contact list, or nearby record. Exclude addresses belonging to senders, recipients, coworkers, assistants, organizations, or unrelated people unless the evidence explicitly assigns that address to the profile subject.

- **Phones:** Find every complete phone number explicitly assigned to the person and preserve it exactly as shown, including punctuation and country code. Ground each number independently. Exclude switchboards, organization-wide lines, fax numbers, numbers belonging to other people, and unlabeled fragments or bare extensions such as `5-1750`. Do not complete or normalize a partial number.

- **Social profiles:** Preserve every exact handle or profile URL explicitly identified as the person’s own account. Ground each account independently. Exclude organization accounts, search or directory pages, article-author navigation links, sharing links, and accounts that merely mention or depict the person. Do not construct handles or URLs from names.

- **City and country:** Extract a location only when the evidence explicitly states that the person lives, resides, is based, or works there. A subject-specific biography or location line may support it; an employer address, organizational headquarters, office directory heading, event venue, dateline, birthplace, jurisdiction, national office, or public role alone may not. Preserve the stated city wording and punctuation without appending an unstated state or country. Keep city and country separate. Normalize an explicit country reference of `U.S.`, `US`, or `USA` to `United States`, but do not infer a country solely from a city, state, employer, domain, office, or governmental role.

- **Pronouns:** Prefer an explicit pronoun declaration and preserve its slash-delimited set. Otherwise, scan all evidence for every distinct pronoun form used unambiguously for the subject; do not stop after the first form. Return the attested forms slash-delimited in subject/object/possessive order, such as `he/him/his` only when all three forms are supported. Do not add unattested forms to complete a paradigm. Exclude pronouns referring to another person, a quoted speaker, a group, or an ambiguously resolved antecedent.

- **Gender:** Populate gender only from direct subject-specific lexical evidence. Explicit `male` or `man` supports `male`; explicit `female` or `woman` supports `female`. Clearly resolved subject-specific use of one or more pronouns from a consistent `he/him/his` paradigm may support `male`, and a consistent `she/her/hers` paradigm may support `female`, provided there is no conflicting or ambiguous evidence. Do not infer gender from a name, image, voice, title, honorific, relationship, occupation, or statistical assumption. Abstain on ambiguity or conflict.

- **Current positions:** Extract every separately supported role that the evidence presents as current in its relevant context. Recognize direct relationship constructions such as serving as a title at an organization, working for an organization, holding an office, belonging to a board, or performing a named role for a program, publication, campaign, or product. Do not overlook a supported role merely because it appears in prose rather than a structured employment field.
  - Pair each title only with the organization, institution, office, board, program, publication, campaign, or product to which that same evidence directly attaches it. Do not borrow a title or company from neighboring text.
  - Preserve the explicit title label. If a phrase combines an organization-identifying modifier with the role, separate the organization from the occupational title rather than duplicating the organization in the title, unless the evidence clearly presents the entire phrase as the formal title. Do not absorb department headers, campaign labels, acronyms, or unrelated descriptors into the title.
  - Use the full organization name when the evidence explicitly supplies it for that relationship. Do not replace it with an acronym, shorten it, expand an acronym from outside knowledge, substitute a parent organization, or choose a nearby department merely because it appears on the same page.
  - Treat roles with different organizations or capacities as distinct. A program or board role and an employment relationship may both be emitted when each is independently supported. Do not collapse them into one pair or substitute a general profession for a specifically stated office or board role.
  - Emit `Employee` only when employment with the organization is explicitly stated and no more specific title is supplied for that employment relationship.
  - Accept present-tense statements and evidence expressly describing a role as current at the evidence’s relevant date. Check for later evidence of departure, replacement, retirement, termination, or an end date. Treat former, previous, then-current, nominated, nominee, candidate, prospective, future, and merely announced roles as non-current.
  - Ground the title and company separately with evidence supporting the same relationship. A passage supporting only the title or only the organization is insufficient for the pair.